  "worked_solution": "First, we write $1\\,\\frac{1}{9}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{1}{9}=\\frac{(1\\times 9)+1}{9}=\\frac{10}{9}\n$$\n\nAlso, we write $-\\frac{1}{3}$ as $-\\frac{3}{9}$ and then we simply subtract the numerators. This gives\n\n$$\n-\\frac{3}{9}-\\frac{10}{9}=\\frac{-3-10}{9}.\n$$\n\nNow, we work out $-3-10$ using a number line:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14148354/assets/f27b92c1bbd62f5cba5fc75a4205034a24fb0298e77a662d6c59203dd42a2760.png)\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{-3-10}{9}=\\frac{-13}{9}=-\\frac{13}{9}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{13}{9}=-1\\,\\frac{4}{9}\n$$",
